From: PWM Backlight Fix
Subject: [PATCH] pwm: sunxi: fix pinctrl being reverted after pin_set_state

The function sunxi_pwm_pin_set_state() calls devm_pinctrl_get(), selects
the requested state, then immediately calls devm_pinctrl_put(). This
causes the pinctrl reference count to drop to zero, which triggers
pinmux_disable_setting() and reverts the hardware mux register back to
gpio_in.

This bug is invisible for PWM channels enabled in the board DTS at boot
(like pwm0_4, pwm1_6, pwm1_8, pwm1_9), because those platform devices
have matching drivers that call pinctrl_bind_pins() at probe time,
maintaining an extra reference.

However, for PWM channels enabled dynamically via DT overlay (like
pwm1_7), the child platform device (e.g., compatible="allwinner,sunxi-pwm17")
has no matching driver, so pinctrl_bind_pins() is never called. The
pinctrl reference obtained by sunxi_pwm_pin_set_state() is the only
reference, and devm_pinctrl_put() releases it, reverting the mux.

Fix by removing the explicit devm_pinctrl_put(). The devm-managed
pinctrl handle is properly released when the device is destroyed.

Signed-off-by: PWM Backlight Fix
---
 bsp/drivers/pwm/pwm-sunxi.c | 1 -
 1 file changed, 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/bsp/drivers/pwm/pwm-sunxi.c b/bsp/drivers/pwm/pwm-sunxi.c
index xxxxxxx..xxxxxxx 100644
--- a/bsp/drivers/pwm/pwm-sunxi.c
+++ b/bsp/drivers/pwm/pwm-sunxi.c
@@ -544,7 +544,6 @@ static int sunxi_pwm_pin_set_state(struct device *dev, char *name)
 	}

 exit:
-	devm_pinctrl_put(pctl);
 	return err;

 }
